The empty terminal result (No hosts found, No IPs found) is caused by a mix of active scraping blocks and how 1win maps its online infrastructure. [1, 2] 
Because 1win.com is a massive global gambling site, they aggressively route their web assets behind strict anti-bot proxies (like Cloudflare Enterprise) and use massive redirect networks to evade detection. Passive scrapers like DuckDuckGo or Yahoo rarely capture their actual internal assets natively anymore. [3, 4, 5] 
To successfully find subdomains and infrastructure details, you need to change your strategy from standard passive search engines to structural or active tools.
## Strategy 1: Use dnsdumpster via theHarvester
The dnsdumpster engine pulls directly from historic DNS datasets rather than parsing web search result pages, making it much more reliable for heavily protected domains. [6] 
Run this command:

theHarvester -d 1win.com -b dnsdumpster

## Strategy 2: Switch to Active Subdomain Brute-Forcing
If passive scraping returns absolutely nothing, you can use theHarvester's built-in active dictionary attack. This forcefully checks a list of common subdomains (e.g., admin.1win.com, api.1win.com) to see which ones actually respond. [6, 7] 
Run this command:

theHarvester -d 1win.com -c


* -c: Tells theHarvester to run a DNS brute-force module against the target domain.

## Strategy 3: Bypass theHarvester with Subfinder or Amass
The community consensus among penetration testing professionals is that modern security distributions rely on specialized tools like subfinder or amass because they map out protected infrastructures significantly better than theHarvester.
You can install and use subfinder directly on your Ubuntu machine right now:

# 1. Install subfinder
sudo apt update && sudo apt install subfinder -y
# 2. Run a clean scan against the target
subfinder -d 1win.com

